9	/** The sheet's settings: the clerk limit with two decimals ('' when there is none) and the floor with two
10	 *  decimals (a claim's live due is at least it), and the terms in business days (a claim's due-by is the terms-th business day after its date: not a Saturday, Sunday or holiday), and
11	 *  the late percent with two decimals (an overdue claim gains, of its figure, the one in force on each full 30-day boundary past its due-by), and the
12	 *  policy date (yyyy-mm-dd; '' when blank: the sheet's today) a claim's live figures round by, and the period numbering from (yyyy-mm-dd; '' when off: on or after it, by
13	 *  the sheet's today, a claim opened is numbered within its period), and each grade's own terms in days ('' when the
14	 *  grade has none: it uses the sheet's terms), and the cap with two decimals ('' when there is none: a person's
15	 *  due-total is at most it), and the proof threshold with two decimals ('' when there is none: a claim whose due is
16	 *  above the one in force on its date cannot be settled or approved without a proof), and the note threshold with two decimals ('' when there is none: a
17	 *  claim whose due is above the one in force on its date cannot be opened with an empty note). A claim's terms are fixed when it is created. Each is the value in force on the sheet's
18	 *  today; `dated` holds every value recorded for the settings that carry a figure, by key (clerk-limit, floor, terms,
19	 *  terms-A/B/C, late-pct, cap, proof-threshold, proof-threshold-A/B/C, note-threshold, rounding, review-threshold), oldest from-date first. `perms` is the permission matrix (not dated): for
20	 *  each action the roles that may take it, comma separated ('' when blank: the standing rule). `graceDays` is the grace days (not dated; '' when off): when the sheet's
21	 *  today is changed, each open period whose to-date plus them is before the new today is closed. `entryDate` is the entry date (not dated; '' when off): while it is set, a new claim
22	 *  given no date is dated it instead of the sheet's today. `gradeGrace` is each grade's grace in days ('' when the grade
23	 *  has none), dated (keys grace-A/B/C): a claim whose person's grade in force on its date has a grace in force on that
24	 *  date is overdue, and counts its late charge, from its due-by plus it. `gradeProofThreshold` is each grade's own proof
25	 *  threshold with two decimals ('' when the grade has none: the sheet's applies), dated (keys proof-threshold-A/B/C). `pot` is the pot
26	 *  with two decimals (not dated; '' when there is none): each member of a balanced pool is shown their cut of it. `parent` is
27	 *  the parent sheet's id (not dated; '' when there is none): a grade with no rate in force on a date on this sheet takes
28	 *  the parent's rate for it on that date, then the parent's parent's, up to three levels up. `fiscalStartMonth` is the
29	 *  month (1 to 12, not dated; 1 unless set) a fiscal year starts on the first day of (see fiscalYearOf). `alertThreshold`
30	 *  is the alert threshold with two decimals (not dated; '' when off): a person whose due-total crosses above it over a
31	 *  change is alerted in the audit file. `rounding` is the rounding mode in force (half-up or half-even; '' when none),
32	 *  dated (key rounding): a claim whose date has one in force rounds its live figures by it, else by the policy date. `reviewThreshold` is the review threshold with two decimals in force ('' when none), dated (key
33	 *  review-threshold): a claim whose due is above the one in force on its date goes to REVIEW when opened. `escalateAfter` is the escalation gap in days (not dated; '' when off): reminding a
34	 *  claim whose previous reminder was at least that many days before the sheet's today escalates it, once ever.
35	 *  `reviewSla` is the review SLA in days (not dated; '' when none): a REVIEW claim whose review day plus it is before
36	 *  the sheet's today is review-late (see reviewLate). `proofKinds` is the proof kinds, comma separated (not dated; '' when
37	 *  none): while set, a claim above the proof threshold needs a proof of each listed kind before it can be settled or
38	 *  approved (the server refuses it). `defaultGrade` is the default grade (A, B or C; not dated; '' when blank, which
39	 *  means A): the quick-add bar gives a new person it, and an import line with a blank grade uses it.
40	 *  `gradeClerkLimit` is each grade's own clerk limit with two decimals ('' when the grade has none: the sheet's
41	 *  applies), dated (keys clerk-limit-A/B/C). */
42	export type Settings = { clerkLimit: string; floor: string; terms: string; latePct: string; policyDate: string;
43	  periodNumberingFrom: string; gradeTerms: Record<string, string>; cap: string; proofThreshold?: string; noteThreshold?: string; dated?: Record<string, Dated[]>;
44	  perms?: Record<string, string>; graceDays?: string; entryDate?: string; gradeGrace?: Record<string, string>;
45	  gradeProofThreshold?: Record<string, string>; pot?: string; parent?: string; fiscalStartMonth?: string; alertThreshold?: string;
46	  rounding?: string; reviewThreshold?: string; escalateAfter?: string; reviewSla?: string; proofKinds?: string;
47	  defaultGrade?: string; gradeClerkLimit?: Record<string, string> };
